[38;5;231m⏺[39m Taking A, then: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m gets the userId and returns it, and the signature stays [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m. The ID
  should come from whatever verified the credentials, not from the caller. That also keeps other forms out of the
  business of passing it around.

  Your answer moves this up a path, though. "Persist" and "other forms will need it later" mean shared state the app
  doesn't have yet: a session/current-user layer that [38;5;153mlogin()[39m writes and other forms read. That's new structure, so I'm
  switching to the [1marchitectural[0m path. I'll ask a few more questions, propose approaches, show you the design in
  sections, and then write a short spec before any code.

  [1mFirst question: where the userId persists.[0m The choice is how long it lasts and who can see it.

  - [1mlocalStorage:[0m survives tab closes and browser restarts until logout, and every tab shares it. The cost is that any
    script on the page can read it. That's fine for a userId (it isn't a secret) but it would be wrong for tokens. Easy
    to change later if access goes through one module.
  - [1msessionStorage:[0m lasts per tab and is cleared when the tab closes. A second tab starts logged out, which surprises
    people in a multi-form app.
  - [1mServer-side session (cookie set by the API):[0m the authoritative and most secure option. But this repo has no backend
    ([38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m points at a stub on example.com), so it depends on work outside this codebase.

  [1mMy recommendation is localStorage behind a small session module[0m ([38;5;153msetCurrentUser[39m / [38;5;153mgetCurrentUser[39m / [38;5;153mclearCurrentUser[39m).
  Forms only ever call the module. If you add a real backend session later, only that module's internals change.

